@style(ECE451_quiz, topic= Example QuestionsParallel Computation)

@h1(C++ Optimization)
@qc(1) For a correct C++ program, an unoptimized program must produce ___ results than optimized. (same/different)
   two reasons for differences: 1. your program is wrong 2. THERE WOULD HAVE TO BE A BUG IN THE compiler

@qc(1) For an incorrect C++ program, can optimization change the observed results? (yes/no)

@qc(1) If a loop's runtime drops from 10 seconds to nearly zero after optimization, what likely happened? (10 words or less)

concrete example:
sum the numbers from 1 to N

compiler knows sum=n(n+1)/2


@qc(1) Why might a compiler remove a loop that takes 10 seconds? (10 words or less)

for games NEVER DO A BUSY LOOP. You ahve to wait()

@qc(1) Why should parallel code keep frequently used values in registers? (10 words or less)
   1. memory is slow compared to registers
   2. it's shared by ALL CORES (that's your bottleneck)

@qc(1) How fast are registers?
you can read 2 registers, add and write back ALL IN ONE clock
Row access time RAS  column access CAS = 130clock DDR5 RAM Burst 16 sequential is fast (TEST #2)

@qc(1) Which is generally faster for repeated access: a register or RAM? (register/RAM)
REGISTER
for future reference (not on test)
L1 cache HIT 4 clock
L2 cache HIT 12
L3 cache HIT 30 (shared by the entire chip)

@qc(1) Which is generally faster: register access or cache-miss memory access? (register/memory)
REGISTER

*IGNORE  @qc(1) What compiler optimization eliminates redundant loads of an unchanged value? (name)

 @qc(1) What compiler optimization removes computations whose results are never used? (name)
DEAD CODE ELIMINATION

@qc(1) What compiler optimization replaces multiplication by a constant power of two with a shift when valid? (name)
* PEEPHOLE OPTMIZATION

ADD  %rax, %rbx #rbx = rbx + rax
LEA  %rax, %rax*4, %rcx # rcx*5

@qc(1) What compiler optimization moves loop-invariant computations outside a loop? (name)
* CODE HOISTING

@qc(1) What compiler optimization reduces function-call overhead by substituting the function body? (name)
inlining

inline double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }


double y = add(2,3); // 2+3 ==> 5

@qc(1) Why can eliminating memory accesses improve parallel performance? (10 words or less)

* @qc(1) What type of dependency prevents two instructions from executing independently? (dependency type)

* @qc(1) If iteration i reads a value written by iteration i-1, what dependency exists? (name)

@qc(1) Can a compiler freely reorder two operations when doing so changes observable behavior? (yes/no)
NO

@qc(1) Does C++ optimization generally preserve the behavior of a well-defined program? (yes/no)

* @qc(1) Which GCC option enables common optimizations without the most aggressive optimization level?
    g++ -O2 optimize but don't get crazy
    g++ -O3 get aggressive (usually no difference)

@qc(1) Can -O3 guarantee that every program runs faster than with -O2? (yes/no)

@qc(1) Why can optimization make debugging harder? (10 words or less)
The debugger no longer steps 1:1 through your code. Pieces no longer exist.
Some variables are optimized out of existence.

@qc(1) If a computed value is never used, what may the optimizer do? (10 words or less)
eliminate it

@qc(1) What is loop unrolling? (10 words or less)
do multiple loops in one iteration to reduce loop overhead

@qc(1) What is the main purpose of loop vectorization? (10 words or less)
do it n at a time

@qc(1) Which can execute multiple data operations per instruction: SIMD or scalar execution? (SIMD/scalar)
Single instruction multiple data
(but modern CPUs try to execute 4 instructions at a time)

@qc(1) Why can register pressure reduce performance? (10 words or less)
running out of registers means we have to read/write to memory (slow)

@qc(1) If registers run out, where might the compiler store temporary values? (location)
STACK

* @qc(1) What is the name for storing register values temporarily in memory? (term)

@qc(1) Does eliminating redundant loads necessarily eliminate all memory accesses? (yes/no)
NO. Our problem may require memory access

@qc(1) Why can a memory-bound loop gain little from arithmetic optimization? (10 words or less)
there isn't enough computation so we are waiting on memory. That is the bottleneck.

@qc(1) If two threads repeatedly modify the same shared variable, what performance problem may occur? (10 words or less)
If two threads DON'T use a mutex, then there will be a RACE CONDITION
if they DO it will be slow

