@h2(AVX Instructions)

@qc(3) An AVX2 YMM register is 256 bits wide. How many 32-bit integers, single-precision floating-point values, and double-precision floating-point values fit in one YMM register?

@qc(3) Two YMM registers contain eight 32-bit integers each. An element-wise packed addition instruction is applied to them. Describe exactly what happens to the eight pairs of values. Does the instruction compute the sum of all eight elements?

@qc(4) A YMM register contains the values `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]`. Describe the result of adding the vector `[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80]` using packed 32-bit integer addition.

@qc(3) Explain the difference between SIMD parallelism and thread-level parallelism. Give one example of each.

@qc(3) An AVX2 program processes eight 32-bit integers per vector instruction. An array contains 1,003 integers. How many complete vectors can be processed, and how many elements remain? Describe one way to handle the remainder safely.

@qc(4) A programmer loads eight floating-point values into a YMM register and performs one packed addition instruction. The programmer expects the result to be the sum of all eight values. Explain the error and describe the additional computation required.

@qc(4) A program uses four threads, and each thread performs AVX2 operations on eight 32-bit elements per packed instruction. Assuming all four threads execute one packed addition instruction concurrently, how many element-wise additions are performed in total? Explain why this does not guarantee a fourfold speedup over a single thread.

@qc(4) Write a short C++ program using AVX2 intrinsics to add two arrays of eight single-precision floating-point values and store the result in a third array. Assume the arrays contain exactly eight elements and the target supports AVX2.

