@h1(OpenMP)

@qc(1) Explain what #pragma does? (10 words or less)


@qc(10) What does this program print? Explain why the output is not necessarily the value a programmer might expect.

```cpp
int sum = 0;

#pragma omp parallel
{
    sum++;
}

std::cout << sum << '\n';
```

@qc(4) Identify the bug in this program and rewrite the OpenMP directive or code to compute the correct sum.

```cpp
int sum = 0;

#pragma omp parallel for
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    sum += i;
}

std::cout << sum << '\n';
```

@qc(3) What is an OpenMP barrier? What must happen before a thread can pass an ordinary barrier?

@qc(3) Explain the difference between `parallel for` and a parallel region containing an ordinary loop. How does work get distributed in each case?

@qc(4) Write a short OpenMP program that computes the sum of the integers from 0 through 999,999. Use a reduction to obtain the correct result.


@qc(1) What does #pragma omp parallel for do? (10 words or less)
@qc(1) What does #pragma omp barrier do? (10 words or less)
@qc(1) What does #pragma omp parallel do? (10 words or less)
@qc(1) Which OpenMP directive creates a team of threads? (directive)
@qc(1) Which OpenMP directive distributes loop iterations among threads? (directive)
@qc(1) Which OpenMP directive synchronizes threads at a barrier? (directive)

@qc(1) What does this program output?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#include <stdio.h>
#include <omp.h>
int main() {
    #pragma omp parallel num_threads(4)
    printf("X");
}
@end

@qc(1) How many times is X printed?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    #pragma omp parallel num_threads(4)
    {
        printf("X");
    }
}
@end

@qc(1) How many times is each loop iteration executed?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    #pragma omp parallel for num_threads(4)
    for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
        printf("%d ", i);
}
@end

@qc(1) How many loop iterations does each thread receive with static scheduling?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#include <omp.h>
int main() {
    #pragma omp parallel for num_threads(4) schedule(static)
    for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++)
        work(i);
}
@end

@qc(1) Which thread executes iteration i=0 with this schedule?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#pragma omp parallel for num_threads(4) schedule(static, 2)
for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
    work(i);
@end

@qc(1) Which thread executes iteration i=5 with this schedule?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#pragma omp parallel for num_threads(4) schedule(static, 2)
for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
    work(i);
@end

@qc(1) How many threads reach the barrier?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#pragma omp parallel num_threads(4)
{
    work();
    #pragma omp barrier
}
@end

@qc(1) What happens if one thread skips this barrier? (short answer)
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#pragma omp parallel num_threads(4)
{
    if (omp_get_thread_num() != 0)
        #pragma omp barrier
}
@end

@qc(1) Is an implicit barrier present at the end of omp parallel for? (yes/no)

@qc(1) Does omp parallel for have an implicit barrier by default? (yes/no)

@qc(1) Which clause sets the number of threads? (clause)
@code(cpp, run, lines=4)
#pragma omp parallel num_threads(6)
work();
@end

@qc(1) What is the value of omp_get_thread_num() for the master thread? (integer)

@qc(1) What values can omp_get_thread_num() return with 4 threads? (range)

@qc(1) How many times does each thread execute this statement?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#pragma omp parallel num_threads(4)
{
    #pragma omp single
    work();
}
@end

@qc(1) Which threads execute the loop body?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#pragma omp parallel for num_threads(4)
for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
    work(i);
@end

@qc(1) Does the following code guarantee that B prints after A? (yes/no)
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#pragma omp parallel num_threads(4)
{
    #pragma omp for
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        work(i);
    printf("B");
}
@end

@qc(1) What directive removes the implicit barrier at the end of a loop? (directive clause)
@code(cpp, run, lines=4)
#pragma omp for nowait
for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
    work(i);
@end

@qc(1) Is this code guaranteed to print 1000? (yes/no)
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
int sum = 0;
#pragma omp parallel for
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
    sum++;
@end

@qc(1) Which clause makes this sum safe? (clause)
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
int sum = 0;
#pragma omp parallel for reduction(+:sum)
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
    sum++;
@end

@qc(1) What is the final value of sum?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
int sum = 0;
#pragma omp parallel for num_threads(4) reduction(+:sum)
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    sum++;
@end

@qc(1) Does each thread have a private copy of i in this loop? (yes/no)
@code(cpp, run, lines=4)
#pragma omp parallel for
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    work(i);
@end

@qc(1) Which clause explicitly makes x private to each thread? (clause)
@code(cpp, run, lines=4)
#pragma omp parallel private(x)
work();
@end

@qc(1) What is the final value of x?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
int x = 10;
#pragma omp parallel num_threads(4) private(x)
{
    x = 5;
}
printf("%d", x);
@end

@qc(1) How many times does work() execute?
@code(cpp, run, lines=8)
#pragma omp parallel num_threads(4)
{
    #pragma omp master
    work();
}
@end

@qc(1) Which directive allows exactly one unspecified thread to execute a block? (directive)
@code(cpp, run, lines=4)
#pragma omp single
work();
@end

@qc(1) Does omp single have an implicit barrier by default? (yes/no)

